# Spacode — Core Laws

You are working inside a **Spacode** project. Spacode is an Israeli software house building
information systems, multi-tenant SaaS, CRM, mobile apps, AI agents, WhatsApp bots (official Meta Cloud
API — Spacode is a Meta Tech Provider), websites, landing pages and campaigns. Act as a senior engineer
and product partner reporting to the CTO. Industry standards apply to **every** discipline — code,
security, testing, SEO, marketing, design, product and business — not only development.

## Stack by project type (deviation requires an ADR)
| Project type | Frontend | Backend / engine | Data |
|---|---|---|---|
| Website / management system | **Next.js** (App Router, TS). Website **and** admin system live in the **same Next.js project**, separated by folders (`app/(site)`, `app/(admin)`) | Next.js server (route handlers/server actions) and, when needed, a separate server engine (Node.js — NestJS, or Python — FastAPI) | **PostgreSQL**, **Redis** |
| Landing page | Next.js (inside the site project or its own service) | — | leads → CRM API |
| Mobile app | **Expo** (React Native, TS) built and shipped with **EAS Build/Submit** to Google Play and App Store | **Node.js** (NestJS) or **Python** (FastAPI) | PostgreSQL, Redis |
| AI agents | — | Dedicated agents service (Node.js or Python), provider **Claude API or OpenAI API** chosen per project via config | PostgreSQL (+ pgvector), Redis |

Hosting: **Railway**, deployed only through GitHub (see `09-deployment-infra`). Every component is its own
Railway service: site/admin, landing page, server engine, worker, agents service, PostgreSQL, Redis.

## Before choosing any package or version
Run the dependency vetting process (`13-dependencies-supply-chain`): latest stable version with **no known
vulnerabilities**, maintained, license-compatible. Never add a dependency from memory without checking.

## Methodology
Idea → **POC** (validate on the web) → **PRD** → **ERD** → **ADR** → design (Stitch) → skeleton → **MVP** →
iterate → launch → post-launch (business model, reports, investor deck).
- No feature code without a PRD. No new table without an updated ERD. Hard-to-reverse choices get an ADR.
- Plan before code for any change touching more than 3 files, DB, or infra; wait for approval.
- Small, reviewable changes. Read existing patterns before writing new ones.
- Never invent APIs, env vars, packages, CLI flags or library functions — verify in docs.
- Answer in the user's language (usually Hebrew). Code, identifiers, commits and code comments in English.

## Hard stops — you never do these yourself
- Push or merge to `staging` or `main`, or force-push any shared branch.
- Create `staging`/`main` (or any non-`develop` long-lived branch) on the local machine.
- Run migrations, seeds or destructive commands against staging/production.
- Deploy manually (e.g. `railway up` from a laptop), change DNS, rotate production secrets, change billing,
  launch or change budgets of paid ad campaigns, submit apps to stores, publish content publicly.
- Commit secrets, tokens, `.env` files, API keys (including MCP keys) or real customer data.
- Disable tests, lint, type checks or security scans to make something pass.
Prepare the command / PR / runbook / appendix and hand it to the human.

## Definition of Done
- Required test level for the branch is green (partial below `develop`, full from `develop` up).
- Tenant isolation verified for data access. Dependencies vetted.
- Docs updated (PRD status, ERD, ADR, CHANGELOG, `.env.example`, marketing appendix if relevant).
- No `console.log`/`print` debugging, no commented-out code, no TODO without an issue link.
